

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where does the persistent person-identifying userId originate? → Server issues it (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Server-issued it is. Next: how long it lives and where it's kept.

  [1mThe fork:[0m this decides whether a returning visitor is still "known" after they close the tab, and — the part that
  bites — whether some later form treats "a userId is present" as "this person is authenticated." A stored ID is an
  identifier, not proof of anything. Whatever we pick, the spec will state that reading the ID is never an auth check.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — lives until the tab closes. Other forms in the same session read it; closing the tab clears it.
    Safest default: an abandoned shared machine doesn't leak the identity, and the short lifetime makes it hard to
    mistake for a durable login. Cost: no "welcome back" across visits.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restarts. Buys cross-visit continuity. Cost: it persists on shared/public machines
    until something explicitly clears it, and a stale ID from months ago looks identical to a fresh one. Needs an
    explicit clear-on-logout path, which this app doesn't have yet.
  - [1mIn-memory module state[0m — a variable in an identity module, gone on reload. Simplest and leaks nothing. Cost: an F5
    loses it, which fails "persist" as you described it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m. It satisfies "persists and other forms can read it" for the lifetime that actually
  matters, without the shared-machine exposure of [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. If you want cross-visit continuity later, moving to
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is a one-line change behind the module boundary — so this is cheap to revisit, which is why I'd take the
  conservative option now.
